{
  "dom_id": "step-q141264",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $5$, respectively, what is the degree of $P+Q?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{5}.$ No ${x}^{5}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{5}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $5.$",
